WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.480
&gt;&gt;&gt; The federal trade commission and 17 states
launching a lawsuit against Amazon.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.730
The suit accuses Amazon of having a uponopoly
over the retail environment and using market

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:18.670
size and power to raise prices for consumers.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:20.610
&gt;&gt; People are paying higher prices.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:27.130
Consumers are paying more, small businesses
are having to pay 50% Amazon tax right now,

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:33.170
and so ultimately the complaint is seeking
to restore the lost promise of competition,

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:39.739
greater competition means lower prices, better
quality, better selection, and greater innovation.

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:42.079
And that's ultimately what this case is about.

00:00:42.079 --> 00:00:47.350
&gt;&gt; The case allegations unfairly promotes
its own platforms and services harming third-party

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:49.960
sellers who rely on Amazon to sell their goods.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:53.440
The claim has followed Amazon for years.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:54.980
[7:57:28 AM]

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:56.359
for years.

00:00:56.359 --> 00:01:01.000
Lena khan wrote about this in 2017 as a lieu
student.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.990
Here is how she explained it to us in 2019.

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:13.119
&gt;&gt; A situation when a single company is able
to set the terms of the marketplace.

00:01:13.119 --> 00:01:18.630
That's a situation where we should worry about
the market not being competitive.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:24.640
&gt;&gt; That year I asked the then-ceo of Amazon
worldwide consumer Jeff Welke about these

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.280
criticisms.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:27.630
Amazon's marketplace dominance.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:31.220
Here's what he said.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:36.200
&gt;&gt; Does Amazon give priority to unprioritize
its private label in search?

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.420
&gt;&gt; We prioritize the things that customers
want.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:45.280
&gt;&gt; A recent hearing on capitol hill an Amazon
attorney went a step further and categorically

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:46.790
denied the claim.

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:50.750
&gt;&gt; We don't use their individual data when
making decisions to launch private brand.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:55.380
Our incentive is to help the seller to succeed
because we rely on them.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:59.590
&gt;&gt; When Wilkie left the company in 2021, Lina
khan told CNN the

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:00.590
[7:58:30 AM]

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:04.920
in 2021, Lina khan told CNN the ftc has not
ruled out holding Amazon executives personally

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:09.410
lived for the company's alleged conduct if
they find sufficient evidence.

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:15.940
Mazon says it could cause the very problems
is hopes to solves and, quote, if the ftc

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:21.470
gets its way, the result would be fewer products
to choose from, higher prices, slower Dallas

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:27.240
and reduce options for small businesses.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.989
This is not the ftc's only lawsuit against
Amazon.

00:02:29.989 --> 00:02:36.010
It's suing several executives alleging schemes
to enroll people into their prime memberships

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:37.520
without knowing.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:41.560
You have been focused on this all morning.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.329
&gt;&gt; Did I email you enough about it?

00:02:44.329 --> 00:02:45.329
&gt;&gt; I love those emails.

00:02:45.329 --> 00:02:46.849
I learn a lot from them.

00:02:46.849 --> 00:02:47.849
What is your take?

00:02:47.849 --> 00:02:52.870
Because 14 a huge moment not just for the
ftc, the Biden administration, and Lina khan.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:54.120
&gt;&gt; This has been her fight.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:03:00.580
She is the first who raised the question to
this country about what a monopoly, do we

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:03.659
have to change the way we define a monopoly
from the Microsoft days

00:03:03.659 --> 00:03:04.659
[7:59:32 AM]

00:03:04.659 --> 00:03:05.709
monopoly from the Microsoft days to what Amazon
is now.

00:03:05.709 --> 00:03:10.159
My question is, it's important to track 17
states attorney general are onboard.

00:03:10.159 --> 00:03:15.549
What does it mean for people at home and who
sell on Amazon.

00:03:15.549 --> 00:03:20.739
If they prevail, is it just going to be fines
and penalties or will actual changes happen

00:03:20.739 --> 00:03:22.230
that benefit them?

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:26.980
Amazon, too, saying if you succeed in what
you are trying to do, it's just going to cost

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:29.209
people more.

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:32.650
It's groundbreaking.

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:39.519
See where it goes.

